{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792692698,"nonce":"qhRI3IHuGL3gJZq3H8aXWAiCREA=","title":"Native non-image draws still ignore ClipShape::Path","message":"Raw-image path clips are handled by scoped issue 265d8222c104b990a1414c416d674d06fa36d841476486acc7428dd5a214850f, completing the remaining path slice of raw-image parent 369499d9d754d8353823d4eb9ff5ff76e5469007b2983e58b3a677f88b5bbc6c. Other native draw kinds retain the existing fallback.\n\nCode audit: get_clip_data(false) returns the no-clip sentinel and ClipType::None when the selected nonrectangular shape is a path. SDF and native tessellated draws call that method; native glyph preparation also consumes its bounds. A native fill_rect under a path therefore records no clip, whereas a raw RGBA image can now carry the prepared path payload. The rgba_path_clip integration fallback control explicitly guards the current bounded scope.\n\nFollow up with offscreen controls for SDF, native paths and glyph/text drawing before extending path clipping across these streams. Preserve the push-time frame, fill rule, rectangular scissor and batch ownership. Existing ABI shape helpers may be reusable for SDF, but native path/glyph layouts and dispatch require a separate audit. Arbitrary stacked nonrectangular intersections and shared ClipPath/CSS enum changes are separate work; the shared enum changes remain owner-gated. No device pixels or performance fixes are claimed by this issue.","files":null}]}